\begin{abstract}
Vision-language-action (VLA) models built on pre-trained vision-language models (VLMs) generalize semantically but are trained mostly on high-level understanding tasks, so they under-emphasize the low-level spatial cues and physical dynamics that precise manipulation needs. Prediction-based policies show the opposite profile: future-frame pre-training improves spatial grounding but lacks semantic robustness. We present UP-VLA, a single autoregressive VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding (MMU) on image-text data and future image prediction (PRE) on robot video, and generates actions conditioned on both a scene description and the predicted future. A unified prompting and attention-masking scheme lets continuous CLIP tokens and discrete VQ-GAN tokens share one Phi-1.5 backbone. On CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D, UP-VLA completes 4.08 tasks in a row on average versus 3.06 for GR-1 (about 33\% higher), and reaches 4.42 versus 4.21 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D. Ablations and real-robot experiments on a Franka Panda show that prediction drives spatial precision and MMU drives generalization to unseen objects.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Large vision-language models are an attractive initialization for robot policies because they bring open-world semantic knowledge. RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt} and OpenVLA \citep{kim24openvla} fine-tune such models to output actions, and PaLM-E \citep{driess2023palm} and EmbodiedGPT \citep{mu2024embodiedgpt} use them for embodied reasoning. However, VLMs are pre-trained largely on understanding tasks such as visual question answering. Recent analyses report that they handle spatial relations and physical dynamics poorly \citep{chen2024spatialvlm,wen2024can,balazadeh2024synthetic,ghaffari2024exploring}, yet these are exactly the cues needed for accurate grasping, cable routing and long-horizon control.

A second family of policies instead learns from prediction. GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing} pre-trains on large-scale video generation, and SuSIE \citep{black2023zero}, UniPi \citep{du2024learning} and 3D-VLA \citep{zhen20243d} use predicted images or goals to guide control. These models capture visual dynamics but are not initialized from semantically rich models, and so generalize less well to new objects and instructions (\cref{fig:compare}).

We argue that the two kinds of pre-training are complementary and should be combined in one model. UP-VLA (\cref{fig:teaser}) trains a single autoregressive backbone on an MMU objective from large-scale image-text data and a future-prediction objective from robot video, and then conditions action generation on both. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose UP-VLA, a VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding and future prediction in one autoregressive framework.
\item We introduce a unified prompting and attention-masking strategy that mixes continuous MMU tokens and discrete predictive tokens in a single LLM.
\item We develop a joint prediction-and-understanding action mechanism that conditions actions on a generated scene description and on predicted future images.
\item UP-VLA reaches an average length of 4.08 on CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D (+33\% over GR-1) and improves real-world performance on both unseen-object and precision tasks.
\end{itemize}

