\section{Related Works}
\paragraph{Language-conditioned robot learning.} Following early work on grounding language in navigation and manipulation \citep{macmahon2006walk,tellex2011understanding,stepputtis2020language}, imitation-learning policies such as BC-Z \citep{jang2022bc}, CLIPort \citep{shridhar2022cliport} and RT-1 \citep{brohan2022rt} condition on instructions and generalize across tasks, and Open X-Embodiment \citep{o2023open} pools data across robots. Learning from play \citep{lynch2020grounding} and reinforcement learning with language \citep{luketina2019survey} are alternative routes. We build on this line but adopt a language-model backbone trained with additional visual objectives.

\paragraph{Vision-language-action models.} VLMs have been used as planners \citep{ahn2022can,huang2022inner,liang2023code} and, more directly, as policies that emit actions: RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt}, OpenVLA \citep{kim24openvla}, $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi_0}, RoboFlamingo \citep{li2023vision} and variants adding traces or hierarchy \citep{zheng2024tracevla,zhang2024hirt}. These inherit semantic knowledge from VLM pre-training that stresses high-level understanding. UP-VLA keeps that understanding signal but adds an explicit future-prediction objective to supply the missing low-level cues.

\paragraph{Prediction-based policies.} Video and image generation can supply dynamics knowledge for control. GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing} pre-trains on video generation, SuSIE \citep{black2023zero} edits images to produce subgoals with a diffusion model \citep{ho2020denoising}, UniPi \citep{du2024learning} plans with text-guided video, and Diffusion Policy \citep{chi2023diffusionpolicy}, 3D Diffuser Actor \citep{ke20243d} and Prediction with Action \citep{guo2024prediction} use diffusion for action. These models are not initialized from VLMs and are weaker at semantic generalization. UP-VLA differs by jointly training semantic understanding with prediction in one backbone.

\paragraph{Unified understanding and generation.} Autoregressive models for image generation \citep{parmar2018image,esser2021taming,sun2024autoregressive}, video generation \citep{kondratyuk2023videopoet} and unified multimodal comprehension and generation \citep{ge2024seed,dong2023dreamllm,xie2024show} show that one transformer can both understand and produce images. We transfer this idea to embodied control and show how to feed the resulting representations into action generation.

\section{Preliminaries}
We consider language-conditioned manipulation: given an instruction $L$ and an observation $O_t$, a policy outputs a chunk of low-level actions $a_{t:t+\Delta t}$. We use three tasks on a shared language model $\pi_\theta$. \emph{Multi-modal understanding} maps an image $I$ and text $L$ to text, $\hat{H}_L=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(I,L)$. \emph{Future prediction} maps $(O_t,L)$ to the future observation, $\hat{H}_{O_{t+\Delta t}}=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L)$. \emph{Action generation} additionally outputs $\hat{H}_{A_{t:t+\Delta t}}$.

Images enter through two pathways. A CLIP-ViT encoder \citep{radford2021learning} followed by an MLP projection $E_1$ gives continuous image tokens $u$ in the language embedding space, as in visual instruction tuning \citep{liu2024visual}. A VQ-GAN encoder $E_2$ \citep{esser2021taming} maps images to discrete codebook tokens $v$; future prediction operates on token indices and a VQ decoder reconstructs images. The language backbone is Phi-1.5 \citep{li2023textbooks}. All encoders are frozen and the LLM is fully fine-tuned.

