\subsection{Unified prompting and attention masks}
\Cref{fig:overview} gives the overall architecture and \cref{fig:attn} the prompting. For MMU the input is \{[MMU], image tokens $u$, language tokens $l$\}. Image tokens precede the text so they can attend to one another, and the model predicts the next language token. For PRE the input is \{[PRE], language tokens $l$, current image tokens $v_t$\}. Image tokens follow the text so each attends to all earlier tokens, and the model predicts future image tokens at aligned positions rather than the next token of the sequence.

\subsection{Joint prediction and understanding for action}
Given $(O_t,L)$, the model first generates a scene description $s=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(O_t,L_{\mathrm{prompt}})$ (e.g., ``describe this image''), embeds additional visual information $E_1(O'_t)$ into language space, and forms the extended instruction $L'=[E_1(O'_t),s,L]$. The PRE branch then jointly outputs future image tokens and action tokens, $(\hat{H}_{O_{t+\Delta t}},\hat{H}_{A_{t:t+\Delta t}})=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L')$, so actions attend to both semantic (MMU) and predictive visual tokens. A lightweight policy head (single-layer attention pooling, MAP, followed by an MLP) maps action tokens to continuous actions, $\hat{a}_{t:t+\Delta t}=\mathrm{MLP}(\mathrm{MAP}(\hat{H}_{A_{t:t+\Delta t}}))$. At test time the same procedure is repeated autoregressively.

\subsection{Objectives and training}
The MMU loss is the language-modeling cross entropy $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MMU}}=-\sum_i \log p_\theta(l_i\mid u,l_{1:i-1})$. The prediction loss is the cross entropy over future image tokens, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PRE}}=-\sum_{j=1}^{M}\log p_\theta(v'_j\mid l,v_{1:M})$. The action loss is $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ACT}}=\lVert\hat{a}_{\mathrm{pos}}-a_{\mathrm{pos}}\rVert_2^2+\mathrm{BCE}(\hat{a}_{\mathrm{end}},a_{\mathrm{end}})$, a regression on position and a binary cross entropy on the gripper/end signal. The total is $\mathcal{L}=\lambda_1\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MMU}}+\lambda_2\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PRE}}+\lambda_3\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ACT}}$ (the values of $\lambda_i$ are not reported in our source material). Training has two stages. Stage 1 mixes MMU data (LLaVA-tuning-665k, \citealp{liu2024visual}) and prediction data (25k demonstrations from Bridge, \citealp{walke2023bridgedata}) with task-specific prompts and masks. Stage 2 fine-tunes actions on downstream robot data with joint prediction-and-understanding prompts while continuing MMU co-training to retain semantic ability.

\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Setup}
\textbf{Simulation.} We use CALVIN \citep{mees2022calvin} in the ABC$\rightarrow$D (train on scenes A, B, C; test on D) and ABCD$\rightarrow$D settings, reporting the success rate for completing 1 to 5 chained tasks and the average length. \textbf{Real world.} A Franka Emika Panda is evaluated with more than 2k demonstrations over 6 skills, on seen tasks, unseen-object grasping and precise operations such as cable routing and small-object grasping, with 20 trials per task (\cref{fig:env}). \textbf{Baselines.} VLA-based: RoboFlamingo \citep{li2023vision}, 3D-VLA \citep{zhen20243d}, RT-1 \citep{brohan2022rt}, and UP-VLA-RT-2, our RT-2-style \citep{brohan2023rt} variant. Prediction-based: GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing}, SuSIE \citep{black2023zero}, Uni-Pi \citep{du2024learning}, 3D Diffuser Actor \citep{ke20243d} and Diffusion Policy \citep{chi2023diffusionpolicy}. UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu is a GR-1-like model with our backbone and no MMU.

