\section{Limitations}
Predicted images contain artifacts such as background colour shifts across scenes, suggesting insufficient visual-generation pre-training; better tokenizers or diffusion modules may help. MMU sometimes misclassifies objects, reflecting backbone and data scale. Joint tasks add sequence length and training cost, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning or structured memory could help. Finally, the model uses 2D images; 3D inputs, multiple views or explicit physics are untested. Several baselines are copied from their papers and only some were reproduced (asterisks in \cref{table1}).

\section{Conclusion}
UP-VLA unifies multi-modal understanding and future prediction in one autoregressive VLA and conditions actions on scene descriptions and predicted futures. It improves CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D from 3.06 (GR-1) to 4.08 and ABCD$\rightarrow$D from 4.21 to 4.42, and ablations attribute spatial gains to prediction and unseen-object gains to MMU. The evidence is limited to CALVIN and a small set of real-robot tasks on one platform, and the limitations above remain open.

